---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import StepsStrip from '../components/StepsStrip.astro';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Is the crop exact?',
    a: 'Yes: the numbers are the truth. The box on the picture is a guide you drag; the four number fields are the exact rectangle that is cut, in pixels from the top-left corner, and the result line prints the exact dimensions that came out. Type the numbers when the crop has to be precise.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What do the shapes (1:1, 4:3, and the rest) do?',
    a: 'They hold the rectangle\'s shape while its size changes: drag the corner or type a width, and the height follows the shape. Free lets the box take any shape. Original lock holds the picture\'s own shape, so the crop is a smaller version of the same proportions.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What format comes out?',
    a: 'The input\'s format, where the browser\'s writer has one: a JPG goes back as a JPG, a PNG as a PNG, a WebP as a WebP. A HEIC photo and other kinds the canvas cannot write come out as a PNG, and the file spec says so before you run. A JPG cannot hold see-through pixels, so if the cropped picture has any, they are painted over white, and the result line says when that happened.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I crop an animated picture?',
    a: 'The still frame is what is cut: an animated GIF or WebP comes out as a still picture of its first frame, in PNG. The animation does not survive a crop here, and the page would rather say that than surprise you.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Where does my picture go?',
    a: 'Nowhere. It is decoded, cut and offered back to you inside this page. Nothing is uploaded.',
  },
];
const faqJson = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })),
});
const howToJson = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'HowTo',
  name: "How to crop a picture",
  step: [
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 1, name: "Pick the picture", text: "Drop a picture on the tray, or click to pick one (JPG, PNG, WebP, HEIC and the other kinds the browser reads). It stays on this device." },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 2, name: "Set the crop", text: "Drag the box to move it, drag its corner to size it, drag anywhere else to draw a new box, or type exact pixels. Pick a shape (1:1, 4:3, 3:2, 16:9, the picture's own shape, or free) to hold the proportions while the size changes." },
    { '@type': 'HowToStep', position: 3, name: "Crop and download", text: "Press 'Crop the picture'. The rectangle is cut at full resolution in your browser, and the result line prints the exact pixel dimensions that came out." },
  ],
});
---

<Base title="Crop image: cut a picture to exact pixels, free · Benchzy" description="Crop a picture in your browser: drag the box, hold a shape (1:1, 4:3, 3:2, 16:9), or type exact pixels. Output in the input's format where the canvas allows, PNG otherwise, stated plainly. Nothing is uploaded.">
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-5xl px-4">
    <section class="mt-8">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-accent-deep">Image tools · edit</p>
      <h1 class="font-display text-4xl font-black mt-2">Crop image</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-lg leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
        Cut the part of the picture you actually need: drag the box over it, or type the
        exact pixels. Shapes hold the proportions while you size it: 1:1 for a square post,
        16:9 for a video frame, the picture's own shape for a clean trim. Everything runs
        in your browser; the picture is never uploaded. Changing a picture's dimensions
        without cutting is the <a class="underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-accent-deep" href="/resize-image/">resize image page</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="ticket mt-6 p-5 sm:p-7" id="cr-tool">
      <div id="cr-tray" class="tray p-6 text-center cursor-pointer" role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Pick a picture">
        <p class="font-display text-xl font-bold">Drop a picture on the tray, or click to pick one</p>
        <p class="font-mono text-xs text-ink-soft mt-2">JPG · PNG · WebP · HEIC · it stays on this device</p>
        <input id="cr-file" type="file" accept="image/jpeg,image/png,image/webp,image/heic,image/heif,image/gif,.jpg,.jpeg,.png,.webp,.heic,.heif,.gif" class="hidden" />
      </div>
      <p class="file-loaded hidden" data-loaded-for="cr-file" role="status"></p>

      <div id="cr-stage" class="relative mt-6 hidden select-none overflow-hidden" style="touch-action:none">
        <canvas id="cr-canvas" class="block w-full border border-line bg-paper-deep"></canvas>
        <div id="cr-box" class="absolute border-2 border-accent" style="box-shadow:0 0 0 9999px rgba(27,38,52,0.35)">
          <div id="cr-handle" class="absolute bg-accent" style="width:20px;height:20px;right:-11px;bottom:-11px;cursor:nwse-resize"></div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="grid gap-6 mt-6 md:grid-cols-[1fr_320px]">
        <div>
          <h2 class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft">Shape</h2>
          <div id="cr-aspects" class="flex gap-2 mt-3 flex-wrap">
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-aspect="free" aria-pressed="true">Free</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-aspect="1:1" aria-pressed="false">1:1</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-aspect="4:3" aria-pressed="false">4:3</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-aspect="3:2" aria-pressed="false">3:2</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-aspect="16:9" aria-pressed="false">16:9</button>
            <button type="button" class="chip" data-aspect="original" aria-pressed="false">Original shape</button>
          </div>
          <h2 class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft mt-6">Exact pixels</h2>
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 mt-3 max-w-md">
            <label class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft">From left (X)
              <input id="cr-x" type="number" min="0" step="1" value="0" class="mt-1 block w-full border border-ink bg-ticket px-3 py-2 font-mono" />
            </label>
            <label class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft">From top (Y)
              <input id="cr-y" type="number" min="0" step="1" value="0" class="mt-1 block w-full border border-ink bg-ticket px-3 py-2 font-mono" />
            </label>
            <label class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft">Width
              <input id="cr-w" type="number" min="1" step="1" value="0" class="mt-1 block w-full border border-ink bg-ticket px-3 py-2 font-mono" />
            </label>
            <label class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft">Height
              <input id="cr-h" type="number" min="1" step="1" value="0" class="mt-1 block w-full border border-ink bg-ticket px-3 py-2 font-mono" />
            </label>
          </div>
          <p class="text-ink-soft text-sm leading-relaxed mt-3 max-w-md">
            The box starts as the whole picture. Drag it to move, drag its orange corner
            to size it, or drag on the picture to draw a new box.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h2 class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-widest text-ink-soft">File spec</h2>
          <dl id="cr-spec" class="mt-2">
            <div class="spec-row"><dt>file</dt><dd>none picked yet</dd></div>
          </dl>
          <p class="action-note">
            The output is written in the input's format where the browser's writer has
            one (JPG, PNG, WebP); other kinds, HEIC included, come out as PNG. The file
            spec names the output format before you run.
          </p>
          <button id="cr-run" class="btn-primary w-full mt-5" type="button" disabled>Crop the picture</button>
          <p id="cr-status" class="font-mono text-sm mt-4 leading-relaxed" aria-live="polite">Pick a picture to begin.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div id="cr-result" class="receipt hidden mt-8">
        <div class="tear pt-5 flex items-center gap-4 flex-wrap">
          <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold">Result</h2>
          <span class="stamp" id="cr-stamp">Done on your device</span>
        </div>
        <p id="cr-achieved" class="mt-3 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl"></p>
        <a id="cr-download" class="btn-primary inline-block no-underline mt-3" href="#" download>Download the cropped picture</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <StepsStrip who="For the part of the picture that matters: a face for a profile, a product for a listing, a square for a post, the exact frame a form or a print asks for." steps={[{ n: '01', t: 'Pick the picture' }, { n: '02', t: 'Drag the box, hold a shape, or type exact pixels' }, { n: '03', t: 'Crop and download' }]} />

    <section class="mt-12 grid gap-8 md:grid-cols-2">
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">How it works</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
          The picture is decoded once by the same image program the convert doors run,
          iPhone HEIC included, and held at full size in the page. The box you drag is a
          guide drawn over a smaller view; the cut itself is made from the full-size
          picture at the exact pixel rectangle the numbers name, and written by the
          browser's own writer. The result line prints the dimensions that actually came
          out, measured on the finished file. The download is how we protect your privacy:
          the program travels to your browser; your picture never travels anywhere.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">Honest limits</h2>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
          A crop keeps only what is inside the box: what is outside is gone from the copy
          (your original file is untouched, of course). The output is a fresh write of the
          cut rectangle, not a byte-copy of part of the original file, so a cropped JPG is
          a new JPG written at a high quality setting, not the original's exact encoding.
          Animated pictures come out as a still of the first frame. And a crop cannot add
          detail: cutting a small piece out of a small picture gives a small picture.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mt-10 mb-4">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold border-b-2 border-ink pb-2">Questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-4 grid gap-4">
        {faq.map((f) => (
          <article class="ticket-soft p-5">
            <h3 class="font-bold">{f.q}</h3>
            <p class="text-ink-soft leading-relaxed mt-1">{f.a}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={faqJson}></script>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={howToJson}></script>
  <script>
    import '../scripts/crop-image.js';
  </script>
</Base>
